# A generic body is type-checked AFTER substitution, so a call inside it can only
# fail at an instantiation -- possibly frames away from the code that caused it.
# The note naming the instantiating call is what makes that error readable, and
# it is load-bearing: docs/architecture.md cites this diagnostic as the reason
# user-extensible constraints (traits) are not needed to get usable errors out of
# structural generics. Nothing pinned it before this fixture.
#
# ONE level of instantiation on purpose. tychoc and tychoc1 agree exactly here,
# and DIVERGE as soon as the generic calls another generic: on a two-level chain
# tychoc emits two frames and tychoc1 emits three (it reports the outermost call
# as well). That divergence is real and unfixed -- see the note in
# docs/internals/FRICTION.md -- so pinning the nested shape here would pin one
# compiler's wording against the other's. This fixture pins the part they agree
# on; a nested fixture belongs with the fix, not ahead of it.
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

struct Name:
    s: string

fn show(p: Point) -> string:
    return "(" + str(p.x) + "," + str(p.y) + ")"

fn render(v: $T) -> string:
    return show(v)

fn main():
    println(render(Name("ada")))
